update public.settings set value = $story$The goal is a story people want to finish, not a summary that fills the time. Tell it like the best dinner-table storyteller you know, not like an encyclopedia read aloud. Every stretch of the lesson should reward the listener's curiosity and give them a reason to keep going.

CHOOSING THE SHAPE. Before outlining, decide which of three shapes the subject naturally is, and build the chapters in that order. Narrative, for events, lives, conflicts, discoveries and scandals: hook, setup, escalation, unexpected turn, resolution. Explainer, for how or why something works, and for everyday objects: mystery, intuition, mechanism, surprise, application. Exploration, for places, eras and broad subjects with no single plot: intriguing premise, context, distinctive perspectives, synthesis. Pick the most natural one. Do not manufacture drama the subject does not have.

THE CENTRAL QUESTION. The angle is the one question the whole lesson answers: the most interesting thing about the subject and what makes it surprising or worth understanding. Why did anyone want this? Who thought it was madness, and why were they almost right? What was at stake, who paid, who got rich, who got hurt? What did it change that we still live with? Choose three to six real discoveries that build toward the answer, and know the most satisfying true conclusion before outlining. Every chapter moves toward that answer.

THE OPENING. The opening is a one minute chapter of its own. It is a hook that delivers something interesting inside that minute, never a teaser that only promises. Start on the strongest verified anecdote, contradiction, mystery, striking fact or consequential moment. Make the subject concrete at once and plant the question. No greeting, no "in today's lesson", no abstract introduction, no long background, and no promise the lesson cannot keep. It is good to close the opening on the central question itself, asked plainly, so the listener leaves the first minute wanting the answer.

CONTEXT, QUICKLY. Within the first two chapters, woven into the telling and never as a block of background before anything happens, the listener learns when and where this is, who these people are, what was normal before, what is at stake, and what kind of story this is. For an explainer, the intuitive problem or the common misconception. For an exploration, the time, place and point of view that make it interesting. Give only the background needed for what comes next and bring in the rest later, when it matters. If the heart of it is a mystery nobody has solved, say so here in plain words and give the best guesses. Never leave the listener wondering for minutes what is going on or why they are being told this.

BEATS. A chapter is a turn in the story, and inside it the telling moves in beats. Each beat is exactly one question, scene, mechanism or discovery. A signature beat, one of the story's great moments, has four parts: a setup or question, a concrete scene or example, a real discovery, and what follows from it, and runs about a minute to a minute and a half. A supporting beat runs about thirty seconds, and background needed only for clarity gets a line. Move to a new beat when the main idea changes, never just to fill time. Each chapter should be worth hearing on its own and still push the larger story forward.

MOMENTUM. Never list information. Bring in something new regularly. Alternate explanation with concrete example. Use a specific detail when it makes the thing vivid. Show consequences and connections. Answer the question the listener is probably asking. Do not repeat context already given. Slow down for what is complex or important and move briskly through the rest. Lead with people, motives, conflict and consequences. Describe how something was physically done only when the how is itself astonishing, and then as one clear picture, not a procedure. Skip anything a listener could have guessed. About once a minute the listener should think: wait, really? Use real unanswered questions to pull forward, but do not hang a cliffhanger on every chapter. Intrigue, not sensationalism.

THE ENDING. The last chapter answers the central question. It resolves the main thread, says what the larger significance is where the facts support it, corrects the popular misconception if there is one, and leaves one memorable insight. It should feel earned, specific and complete. Never end on "history teaches us", "and that is why this is fascinating", a thank you, or a list of what was covered.

FOR THE EAR. Conversational prose, clear sentences of varied length, natural transitions, little jargon, nothing that sounds like an essay. Never stack dates, numbers or proper names close together. Never announce a transition ("in this next chapter"). The chapters should play as one continuous telling.

TRUTH FIRST. Engagement never outranks accuracy. Do not invent dialogue, scenes, motives or sensory details. Do not overstate how certain the record is. Keep documented fact apart from interpretation, keep the important qualifications, and name a popular myth as a myth when the evidence is against it. Do not force a twist where there is none. When the sources are thin, tell a narrower story that is true.

THE LESSON TITLE. Name the single most ridiculous true thing in the story as a thing, not as a sentence. The shape is "The [someone or something] that [did the absurd thing]", with "who" for a person. Good titles: "The disco explosion that forfeited a baseball game", "The warship that sank on its first voyage", "The pope who put a dead pope on trial", "The airline with a waitlist for the moon", "The mummy that received military honors". Do not write a full sentence that starts with "A" or "An": "A warship sank on its first voyage" is the wrong shape, and "The warship that sank on its first voyage" is the right one. When that shape truly does not fit, a "Why" title or a possessive is fine: "Why we drive on the right side of the road", "Australia's great emu war". It must grab someone who has never heard of the subject, so never lean on a name, place or event they would not already know. "The Mad Gasser of Mattoon" and "Tunguska: the explosion that left no meteorite" mean nothing to a stranger. Say what happened instead: "The gasser a town feared and never found", "The explosion that flattened a forest and left no crater". Short enough to read at a glance on a small card: nine words at most and never more than 50 letters and spaces. One subject, one absurd thing. Cut every "and", every second clause and every explanation. No colon, no subtitle, capitals only where a sentence would have them. It must be literally true according to the fact notes.$story$ where key = 'story_brief';
